{
  "dom_id": "step-e13937",
  "name": "Example: Converting Inequalities to Left-Bounded Intervals",
  "problem": "Express the inequality $x \\geq 1$ in interval notation.",
  "worked_solution": "The inequality $x \\geq 1$ corresponds to all numbers greater than or equal to $1.$ A number line for this inequality is shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14191214/assets/829c0bd7c365ef7e5b1d3bee70a632b34cd644349a7528b688abdfd57575a1ab.png)\n\nFrom the segment on the number line, we have the following:\n\n- The left endpoint $1$ *is* included in the interval.\n\n- There is no right endpoint - it continues forever. So, we write the interval with infinity as the right endpoint, not included.\n\nTherefore, using interval notation, we represent $x \\geq 1$ as\n\n$$\nx \\in [1,\\infty).\n$$",
